-- chem/fluxsplit.hypercubical — the electric energy of whole-number flux on a graph, split into its two parts. A flux n
-- puts a whole number on every line; Gauss's law fixes its divergence at every vertex to the charge there. Let h be
-- the gradient flux with the same divergence, h_ab = φ_a − φ_b (chem/charge.hypercubical's least energy is Σ h²).
--   fs-k4:    Σ n² − Σ h² − Σ (n − h)² = 2 Σ_v φ_v (div n − div h)_v      (every line of the complete graph on four
--             vertices, four free potentials and six free fluxes; summation by parts)
-- so when the divergences agree, Σ n² = Σ h² + Σ (n − h)²: the energy is the charges' own part Σ h², which is
-- `field-energy`, plus the energy of n − h, a flux without divergence. The fluxes with one divergence differ by
-- whole-number circulations, so n − h runs over one coset of the circulations; the coset is the class of the
-- charges in the graph's group of order `spanning-trees` (the charges modulo the Laplacian's image).
-- On a ring of N lines with unit charges d lines apart the fluxes are m on one arc and m − 1 on the other:
--   fs-ring:  N (d m² + (N − d)(m − 1)²) = (N m − (N − d))² + d (N − d)
-- the charges' part is d(N − d)/N (the two arcs in parallel) and the coset is m − (N − d)/N, the class d in Z/N.
-- With the electric term alone the least energy over whole m is the shorter arc, min(d, N − d) (`fs-hexagon`): a
-- string, growing with distance. The part d(N − d)/N is reached only as the circulation's coset is spread, which
-- is what the loop term does (chem/magloop.hypercubical). A leaf: no module imports it.
import "algebra.hypercubical"
import "charge.hypercubical"

